{
  "dom_id": "step-q201",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A triangle has sides $a$, $b$, and $c.$ The sides $a$, $b$, and $c$ are of lengths  $9$, $12$, and $18$, respectively. What is the measure of the angle between the sides $b$ and $c?$ Round your answer to two decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14249653/assets/dede0326d992509dedf1c3a2854b47076ccc48ebf0e07705c8c12762d8ecdead.png)\n\nWe use the Law of Cosines to solve for angle $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡A & =\\frac{{b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-{a}^{2}}{2bc}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\na=9,\\,b=12,\\,c=18.\n$$\n\nThis gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡A & =\\frac{{12}^{2}+{18}^{2}-{9}^{2}}{2(12)(18)} \\\\  & =\\frac{144+324-81}{432} \\\\  & =\\frac{387}{432}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠F & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{387}{432}) \\\\  & ={26.38}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
